 ### [Early Splat Termination](811-early-splat-termination.md)
-An optimization that skips the processing of voxels when the accumulated opacity at the target screen pixels is already full, analogous to early ray termination in ray casting. It sets itself apart from sibling methods like [Splat Size Culling](2849-splat-size-culling.md) by operating on accumulated opacity rather than geometric footprint size, making it effective for opaque regions but costly for low-opacity scenes. When to choose: Use for scenes with high opacity or significant occlusion where pixel coverage reaches full opacity quickly. See [Early Splat Termination](811-early-splat-termination.md).
+An optimization that skips the processing of voxels when the accumulated opacity at the target screen pixels is already full, analogous to early ray termination in ray casting [G]. It sets itself apart from sibling methods like [Splat Size Culling](2849-splat-size-culling.md) by operating on accumulated opacity rather than geometric footprint size, making it effective for opaque regions but costly for low-opacity scenes [G]. When to choose: Use for scenes with high opacity or significant occlusion where pixel coverage reaches full opacity quickly. See [Early Splat Termination](811-early-splat-termination.md).
 
 ### [Splat Size Culling](2849-splat-size-culling.md)
-A staging optimization that discards primitives smaller than a defined threshold before rasterization. It trades visual fidelity for real-time frame rates by reducing the workload of splats and fragments generated during the rendering pipeline. It differs from sibling methods like [Early Splat Termination](811-early-splat-termination.md) by operating at the data staging phase rather than during the per-pixel compositing phase, allowing it to prevent expensive rasterization and memory allocation for negligible contributions entirely. When to choose: Use for scenes with many small, negligible splats where performance is prioritized over fine detail. See [Splat Size Culling](2849-splat-size-culling.md).
